107
00:07:41,260 --> 00:07:44,620
And I want you to drift into your own daydream.

108
00:07:44,620 --> 00:07:48,900
Just for a second, what would you do if you won the lottery?

109
00:07:48,900 --> 00:07:51,700
We could rephrase it.

110
00:07:51,700 --> 00:07:57,140
What would you do if your work paid you the same salary they're paying you now to not come into work?

111
00:07:57,140 --> 00:08:04,780
If you had just an excess of money or if you just had an excess of time, what would you do?

112
00:08:05,640 --> 00:08:07,880
What about a position of power or influence?

113
00:08:07,880 --> 00:08:13,500
Is there a position, a leadership position or a role that you dream about being in?

114
00:08:13,500 --> 00:08:20,040
If you didn't have to do the work to get there, if somebody just magically plopped you in that role, which role would you select?

115
00:08:21,560 --> 00:08:27,480
Now, I think this experiment will help us pinpoint exactly what we most value in our lives.

116
00:08:28,180 --> 00:08:30,860
I read a quote as I was preparing for this message.

117
00:08:30,860 --> 00:08:32,240
I've been chewing on it ever since.

118
00:08:32,240 --> 00:08:33,120
It says,

119
00:08:33,120 --> 00:08:40,740
Every choice a person makes is somehow guided by their search for ultimate fulfillment, for value.

120
00:08:40,740 --> 00:08:47,460
We all have this idea of what we want our life to be, this value that we're placing in front of our lives.

121
00:08:47,460 --> 00:09:02,320
And whether we realize it or not, directly or indirectly, every decision that we make, small and big, is aimed at achieving, accomplishing, grabbing hold of the thing that we ultimately value the most.

122
00:09:03,820 --> 00:09:10,840
Whatever we, as individuals, define as ultimate value or the aim for our lives will trickle down to every decision we make.

123
00:09:10,840 --> 00:09:15,400
And our two parables this morning today are aimed at that very question.

124
00:09:15,400 --> 00:09:20,800
What Jesus wants us to wrestle with is what is the ultimate value in our life?

125
00:09:20,800 --> 00:09:24,760
What is the thing that we are striving for with everything that we have?

126
00:09:24,880 --> 00:09:38,020
Sometimes we might not fully realize it, but there is something that we have agreed to, believed that if we get it, it will give us the satisfaction and the value that we're looking for.

127
00:09:38,020 --> 00:09:39,700
And Jesus wants us to wrestle with this question.

128
00:09:39,700 --> 00:09:40,440
What is it?

129
00:09:40,440 --> 00:09:44,820
And will it deliver the results that we think it will, that we're hoping it will?

130
00:09:44,820 --> 00:09:54,860
And then ultimately, the backdoor question that Jesus is going to throw behind the window is what if there's something immensely more valuable available to you?

131
00:09:54,880 --> 00:09:58,020
Would you be willing to consider it?

132
00:09:58,020 --> 00:10:05,200
Would you be willing to set aside all your other values for something of utmost value?

133
00:10:05,200 --> 00:10:08,900
That's the question we're going to wrestle with today.

134
00:10:08,900 --> 00:10:12,220
And now is when I no longer give you permission to daydream, all right?

135
00:10:12,220 --> 00:10:13,840
Daydreaming is over.

136
00:10:13,840 --> 00:10:14,720
I need you to lock in.

137
00:10:14,720 --> 00:10:15,220
It's time.

138
00:10:15,220 --> 00:10:18,020
We're going to read our parables.

139
00:10:18,020 --> 00:10:24,860
And what I want to do right now is I want to read these parables three times slowly before we start getting into the Bible.

140
00:10:24,880 --> 00:10:28,440
The explanation and application of the text.

141
00:10:28,440 --> 00:10:32,240
Because what we have to realize is that when the parables were taught, they weren't taught like a sermon.

142
00:10:32,240 --> 00:10:35,300
They weren't taught and broken down in the moment.

143
00:10:35,300 --> 00:10:40,400
They were given as a story to capture our imagination, as a story that we would take with us.

144
00:10:40,400 --> 00:10:45,800
As the audience left Jesus, he wanted this story just going around in their minds and their hearts.

145
00:10:46,500 --> 00:10:49,280
And so I want that experience for us just for a moment.

146
00:10:49,280 --> 00:10:54,820
But we're Western, so I will explain it to the best of my ability after that.

147
00:10:54,820 --> 00:10:57,680
But first, let's just get this into our hearts and our imaginations.

148
00:10:57,680 --> 00:10:59,160
If you want to, close your eyes.

149
00:10:59,160 --> 00:11:01,200
If that's something that would help you, it would help me.

150
00:11:01,200 --> 00:11:02,580
But I have to read, so I can't.

151
00:11:02,580 --> 00:11:04,840
Let's go ahead.

152
00:11:04,840 --> 00:11:07,500
It's Matthew chapter 13, starting in verse 44.

153
00:11:08,500 --> 00:11:17,840
When a man found it, he hid it again.

154
00:11:17,840 --> 00:11:23,420
And then in his joy, he went and sold all that he had and he bought that field.

155
00:11:23,420 --> 00:11:28,240
Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a merchant looking for fine pearls.

156
00:11:28,520 --> 00:11:34,600
When he found one of great value, he went away and he sold everything he had and bought it.

157
00:11:34,600 --> 00:11:39,500
The kingdom of heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field.

158
00:11:39,500 --> 00:11:42,180
When a man found it, he hid it again.

159
00:11:42,180 --> 00:11:47,700
And then in his joy, he went and sold all that he had and he bought that field.

160
00:11:47,700 --> 00:11:53,000
Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a merchant looking for fine pearls.

161
00:11:53,760 --> 00:12:00,840
When he found one of great value, he went away and sold everything that he had and bought it.

162
00:12:00,840 --> 00:12:07,860
The kingdom of heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field.

163
00:12:07,860 --> 00:12:11,100
When a man found it, he hid it again.

164
00:12:11,100 --> 00:12:19,880
And then in his joy, he went and he sold all that he had and he bought the field.

165
00:12:20,820 --> 00:12:25,880
Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a merchant looking for fine pearls.

166
00:12:25,880 --> 00:12:32,880
When he found one of great value, he went away and he sold everything that he had and he bought it.

167
00:12:32,880 --> 00:12:35,260
It's the word of the Lord.

168
00:12:35,260 --> 00:12:37,820
You guys are getting better.

169
00:12:37,820 --> 00:12:39,500
Thanks be to God.

170
00:12:39,500 --> 00:12:40,920
We'll get there one day.

171
00:12:41,940 --> 00:12:52,200
These two short, similar stories give a holistic picture of how the kingdom of heaven is to be seen and to be received by those that Jesus is announcing it to.

172
00:12:52,200 --> 00:12:58,080
Last week, Corey gave us a really clear and short definition of what the kingdom of heaven is.

173
00:12:58,080 --> 00:13:04,580
The kingdom of heaven, he said, is the rule and reign of God breaking into the world through Jesus.

174
00:13:05,580 --> 00:13:06,460
This is the kingdom.

175
00:13:06,460 --> 00:13:12,740
It's not primarily a message about where you go when you die or what value God can add to your life right now.

176
00:13:12,740 --> 00:13:21,020
The kingdom of heaven is about submitting your life to a king who brings resurrection to this broken and dying world.

177
00:13:21,020 --> 00:13:29,640
And we could add to that definition or adapt it a little bit for our message this week because our message is about receiving the kingdom.

178
00:13:29,640 --> 00:13:30,640
I would say it this way.

179
00:13:30,640 --> 00:13:38,160
The kingdom of heaven is an invitation to submit to the rule and reign of God breaking into the world through Jesus.

180
00:13:38,160 --> 00:13:40,780
This is what we're being placed before us.

181
00:13:40,780 --> 00:13:42,840
This is what Jesus is setting down before us.

182
00:13:42,840 --> 00:13:53,860
It's an invitation to accept God's rule and his reign over our lives because we believe that he is bringing about redemption, that he is bringing in the reign of God through Jesus.

183
00:13:53,860 --> 00:13:56,400
This is the invitation that Jesus offers.

184
00:13:56,400 --> 00:14:04,740
And anybody that tells you any other invitation from Jesus is focusing on smaller elements of the invitation.

185
00:14:04,860 --> 00:14:12,780
But this is the holistic invitation of Jesus, that the rule and reign of God is breaking into the world through Jesus, and you're invited to participate.

186
00:14:12,780 --> 00:14:28,280
Now, in both of our stories, I see four primary activities that the participants of those uncovering the treasure of God's kingdom participate in.

187
00:14:28,280 --> 00:14:30,780
And that's going to be the framework for the rest of our time together.

188
00:14:30,780 --> 00:14:32,620
Four activities.

189
00:14:32,760 --> 00:14:33,740
The first is finding.

190
00:14:33,740 --> 00:14:40,640
If we look at the text, we can see that both individuals in these stories find the kingdom of heaven.

191
00:14:40,640 --> 00:14:47,140
One is actively searching for it while the other seems to accidentally stumble upon it.

192
00:14:47,140 --> 00:14:51,340
But they both find something of immense value.

193
00:14:51,340 --> 00:14:57,800
Now, we don't know exactly why this man walking through a field is walking through a field.

194
00:14:58,600 --> 00:15:05,120
He seems to be out for a nice afternoon stroll, relatively content with his life as it is.

195
00:15:05,120 --> 00:15:10,700
So content with his life as it is that he's just nonchalantly trespassing on someone else's property.

196
00:15:10,700 --> 00:15:17,160
He's just, he's so, like, content with the world that he's just like, oh, property lines, boundary lines.

197
00:15:17,160 --> 00:15:19,480
Man made.

198
00:15:19,640 --> 00:15:20,420
Who needs those?

199
00:15:20,420 --> 00:15:25,600
And he stumbles upon a treasure in this field.

200
00:15:25,600 --> 00:15:29,340
And to us, to our Western minds, this sounds insane.

201
00:15:30,300 --> 00:15:39,760
Because we don't typically bury treasures, although my grandparents, I think, have some coffee cans somewhere that hopefully they wrote down where they're at.

202
00:15:39,760 --> 00:15:47,140
This isn't a typical experience of us with banks, but this was a common experience for the people of this day.

203
00:15:47,240 --> 00:15:51,100
They would have buried valuable treasures on their property.

204
00:15:51,100 --> 00:16:00,000
And because there was so much turmoil at this time, because there was so much war, so many invasions, so much land being taken and stolen,

205
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:05,840
it would be common, somewhat common, for there to be treasure that someone doesn't know exists,

206
00:16:05,840 --> 00:16:11,240
because the person who put it there is long gone or that land isn't in their possession anymore.

207
00:16:12,020 --> 00:16:25,240
And so this seemingly content man on an afternoon stroll comes across a treasure that causes him to liquidate everything that he owns to buy the field and receive the lost treasure.

208
00:16:25,240 --> 00:16:32,120
And we shouldn't get stuck on this morality question of whether or not he should have told the owner of the field that the treasure was there.

209
00:16:32,120 --> 00:16:34,860
The owner didn't know the treasure was there, therefore it wasn't his treasure.

210
00:16:34,860 --> 00:16:37,380
Totally legal in this day, fine, whatever.

211
00:16:37,380 --> 00:16:39,500
You can wrestle with it all you want.

212
00:16:39,620 --> 00:16:45,140
But all I'm saying is if I walk into Goodwill next week and there's a Scotty Cameron putter for sale for $5,

213
00:16:45,140 --> 00:16:49,160
I'm not telling the owners of Goodwill what they have.

214
00:16:49,160 --> 00:16:51,680
I'm just giving them the five bucks and walking away, right?

215
00:16:51,680 --> 00:16:59,140
So this seemingly content man comes across a treasure that he wasn't looking for.

216
00:16:59,140 --> 00:17:03,220
But the moment he comes across it, he's willing to liquidate everything he owns.

217
00:17:03,220 --> 00:17:08,480
The merchant, on the other hand, is actively searching for a pearl of great value.

218
00:17:08,560 --> 00:17:10,960
In this day, the pearl would have been the most valuable jewel.

219
00:17:10,960 --> 00:17:11,940
For us, think diamond.

220
00:17:11,940 --> 00:17:14,220
This is what they would have thought in their minds.

221
00:17:14,220 --> 00:17:16,440
This is the most valuable jewel of the day.

222
00:17:16,440 --> 00:17:22,140
And he spends his life, his job is actively looking for valuable pearls.

223
00:17:22,140 --> 00:17:29,100
And he comes across one in his estimation that is the most valuable pearl that he will ever come across in the rest of his life.

224
00:17:29,140 --> 00:17:36,780
If he were to continue to search from this day on until he dies, he thinks he will not find a pearl more valuable than this one.

225
00:17:38,280 --> 00:17:41,300
And he sells everything he has to buy it.

226
00:17:41,300 --> 00:17:52,920
And I think all of us in the room can resonate with one of these two characters on one level or another when it comes to our search for ultimate fulfillment, for value.

227
00:17:53,880 --> 00:17:56,780
Maybe you're here and you're relatively chill.

228
00:17:56,780 --> 00:18:00,280
You're relatively content with your life as it is.

229
00:18:00,280 --> 00:18:02,520
You've got a decent life, a happy family.

230
00:18:02,520 --> 00:18:03,700
Your daily needs are met.

231
00:18:03,700 --> 00:18:05,300
You're a little stressed.

232
00:18:05,300 --> 00:18:07,720
But all in all, you don't have much to complain about.

233
00:18:08,280 --> 00:18:11,680
And you're not giving much thought beyond today.

234
00:18:11,680 --> 00:18:16,620
You're not wrestling with the difficult questions of life and purpose.

235
00:18:16,620 --> 00:18:17,440
Why are you here?

236
00:18:17,440 --> 00:18:18,580
You're just like, yeah, life's good.

237
00:18:18,580 --> 00:18:29,560
But others of us are like the merchant who we've been on a search for a long time, trying to figure out why we're here.

238
00:18:29,560 --> 00:18:31,560
What is this life all about?

239
00:18:31,560 --> 00:18:32,580
Why does it matter?

240
00:18:32,580 --> 00:18:35,180
What value can I add?

241
00:18:35,180 --> 00:18:36,600
What value can I receive?

242
00:18:36,800 --> 00:18:39,760
We are searching, actively searching.

243
00:18:39,760 --> 00:18:44,980
And maybe you've tried just about everything there is this world has to offer.

244
00:18:44,980 --> 00:18:51,260
Maybe some things have worked better than others, but you still haven't found what you're looking for.

245
00:18:51,260 --> 00:18:59,400
And I still haven't found what I'm looking for.

246
00:18:59,400 --> 00:18:59,880
Thank you.

247
00:18:59,880 --> 00:19:00,280
Thank you.

248
00:19:02,460 --> 00:19:11,220
Whatever character you wrestled with most or whatever character you were most similar to before surrendering to Jesus,

249
00:19:13,060 --> 00:19:17,100
I think that the reality is the next step for both of them is the same.

250
00:19:17,100 --> 00:19:26,000
Whether they stumbled upon it or actively were looking for it, the second step is they saw the value in the treasure.

251
00:19:27,980 --> 00:19:35,040
One person stumbled upon the value, one person was searching for it, but both immediately realized what they had when they found it.

252
00:19:35,040 --> 00:19:39,060
It was something worth giving everything up for.

253
00:19:40,820 --> 00:19:49,700
My hunch, as a pastor who does life with many of you, who regularly has conversations about deep meaning and value and purpose,

254
00:19:49,700 --> 00:19:52,820
my hunch is this is the sticking point for many of us.

255
00:19:54,500 --> 00:20:01,640
Whether you're actively following Jesus, whether you're a fan of his teachings, or whether you're a curious bystander just trying to figure out what he's all about,

256
00:20:01,640 --> 00:20:09,940
the decision Jesus is confronting his audience with is, do you value the kingdom of heaven as the ultimate value in your life?

257
00:20:09,940 --> 00:20:14,700
Even if it doesn't look like what you expected it to look like.

258
00:20:15,860 --> 00:20:19,320
This is the surprising arrival element of the kingdom.

259
00:20:19,320 --> 00:20:27,080
See, at this point in time, most of the Jews in Jesus' audience, they were anticipating God's kingdom to come.

260
00:20:27,080 --> 00:20:28,540
They had prophecies about it.

261
00:20:28,540 --> 00:20:38,980
They had an entire history of stories that were all leading up to the climax of God coming in and breaking Israel free from its oppression.

262
00:20:38,980 --> 00:20:45,920
They were anticipating God's kingdom, but they were primarily anticipating it to come with power and to immediately benefit their physical lives.

263
00:20:45,920 --> 00:20:55,700
They were expecting for God to show up in unimaginably clear ways and for him to bring about his kingdom once and for all.

264
00:20:55,700 --> 00:21:03,700
But what Jesus was teaching about was the kingdom that they were expecting, but it was coming in a way that was different than they expected it.

265
00:21:05,920 --> 00:21:11,600
He didn't roll the treasure into the city streets and open it up for everyone to see.

266
00:21:11,600 --> 00:21:14,700
It's more like a treasure hidden in a field.

267
00:21:14,700 --> 00:21:19,420
It comes in unexpected ways with upside down value systems.

268
00:21:19,420 --> 00:21:24,240
It's a kingdom where the last are first, where true life is found through sacrificial love,

269
00:21:24,240 --> 00:21:29,840
and where those who receive it, and this is what they were not expecting and did not know what to do with it,

270
00:21:29,840 --> 00:21:33,640
that those who received the kingdom would be persecuted and even killed for it.

271
00:21:34,760 --> 00:21:40,700
And yet, Jesus is saying, it is immeasurably more valuable than anything else you could build your life on.

272
00:21:40,700 --> 00:21:47,800
Because the kingdom of heaven is an unstoppable force that overcomes the power of sin and darkness

273
00:21:47,800 --> 00:21:52,580
and will provide the recipient with abundant life now, no matter your circumstances,

274
00:21:52,580 --> 00:21:55,520
and eternal life in the future.

275
00:21:55,520 --> 00:21:58,760
It's like a mustard seed planted in the ground.

276
00:22:01,540 --> 00:22:03,560
And the plant overcomes everything.

277
00:22:03,560 --> 00:22:05,120
It's small.

278
00:22:05,120 --> 00:22:06,460
It's seemingly insignificant.

279
00:22:06,460 --> 00:22:07,960
But it is unstoppable.

280
00:22:07,960 --> 00:22:13,280
This is what Jesus is saying the kingdom of heaven is like.

281
00:22:13,280 --> 00:22:19,980
And he's inviting his audience to decide whether or not they will value it the way that he is valuing it.

282
00:22:22,480 --> 00:22:30,280
He's placing it on the table and saying, this kingdom that I'm preaching about is worth more than anything else you could ever pursue.

283
00:22:30,280 --> 00:22:33,280
And he's giving us the option to consider it and to choose.

284
00:22:34,540 --> 00:22:39,720
And many of the people in the audience would say, this is not what I thought the kingdom of heaven was.

285
00:22:39,720 --> 00:22:41,940
This is not what I was expecting.

286
00:22:41,940 --> 00:22:43,580
I'm out.

287
00:22:45,720 --> 00:22:49,140
But these two characters in our parables, they see the value.

288
00:22:49,140 --> 00:22:55,420
And the question then for us becomes, do we truly understand what the kingdom of heaven is?

289
00:22:55,420 --> 00:22:57,460
The kingdom of heaven that Jesus brings?

290
00:22:57,460 --> 00:23:00,720
Because there's plenty of redefinitions available to us today.

291
00:23:00,720 --> 00:23:05,940
There's plenty of people that come in the name of Jesus, that come under the banner of the kingdom of heaven,

292
00:23:05,940 --> 00:23:07,920
and will offer you all kinds of things.

293
00:23:09,120 --> 00:23:11,700
But what is the kingdom that Jesus promises?

294
00:23:11,700 --> 00:23:14,380
And what does it require of us?

295
00:23:14,380 --> 00:23:24,280
And do we understand the kingdom as it's offered by Jesus and not some repackaging that makes it easier and more simple for us to grab a hold of?

296
00:23:24,280 --> 00:23:25,840
Maybe something that we can put in our pocket.

297
00:23:25,840 --> 00:23:28,900
Something that we have to give up Sunday morning for.

298
00:23:28,900 --> 00:23:33,620
This is not the kingdom that Jesus comes to bring.

299
00:23:33,620 --> 00:23:37,800
The kingdom that Jesus comes to bring infiltrates every aspect of your life.

300
00:23:38,300 --> 00:23:41,540
Changes your entire value system upside down.

301
00:23:41,540 --> 00:23:45,980
Do we truly understand the kingdom that Jesus comes to offer?

302
00:23:45,980 --> 00:23:50,680
And do we see it as more valuable than anything else this world has to offer?

303
00:23:50,680 --> 00:23:51,900
And I'll be honest with you.

304
00:23:51,900 --> 00:23:55,980
It looks foolish to the world.

305
00:23:55,980 --> 00:24:04,680
But to us who are being saved, it is the very power of God for salvation.

306
00:24:04,680 --> 00:24:07,920
The kingdom is not a virtuous add-on.

307
00:24:07,920 --> 00:24:10,580
It's not something we leverage for our own personal gain.

308
00:24:10,580 --> 00:24:13,600
It's not a morality we embrace to make us feel righteous.

309
00:24:13,600 --> 00:24:17,420
It's not a self-help framework that gives us a well-rounded life.

310
00:24:19,760 --> 00:24:26,940
And this, I'm afraid, is what many in the church in America view the kingdom as.

311
00:24:26,940 --> 00:24:29,340
It's a nice thing to add on to our lives.

312
00:24:29,340 --> 00:24:31,080
It's hope for the future.

313
00:24:31,080 --> 00:24:37,780
It's something that makes us feel a little better about ourselves when we go to sleep at night.

314
00:24:37,780 --> 00:24:40,220
But that's not the kingdom that Jesus is offering.

315
00:24:40,220 --> 00:24:48,720
He's offering a kingdom of total surrender, of laying down everything else you value in your life, and letting God's values dictate everything that you do.

316
00:24:48,720 --> 00:24:50,200
It's not easy.

317
00:24:50,200 --> 00:24:51,540
It's not comfortable.

318
00:24:51,760 --> 00:24:52,820
But it's worth it.

319
00:24:52,820 --> 00:24:56,500
I love how Tim Keller says it.

320
00:24:56,500 --> 00:24:59,220
He says, religious people find God useful.

321
00:24:59,940 --> 00:25:02,900
But kingdom people find God beautiful.

322
00:25:02,900 --> 00:25:18,460
And how often do we redefine the kingdom in a way something that we can use for our own benefit, for our own gain, something that we can add to our lives to leverage our own status, our own worth, our own value.

323
00:25:20,360 --> 00:25:24,660
And the kingdom that Jesus offers us is far more beautiful than that.

324
00:25:24,660 --> 00:25:40,160
It's one that with joy we can be willing to lay down every other pursuit of our life in order to pursue this one thing, this thing that sacrifices our own desires for the sake of others, to love God with our whole heart, mind, soul, and strength, and to love our neighbor as ourself.

325
00:25:40,160 --> 00:25:46,960
Both the merchant and the man in the field immediately realize the treasure is worth the sacrifice.

326
00:25:47,200 --> 00:25:49,840
And the third activity they do is they give up everything.

327
00:25:49,840 --> 00:25:59,300
With joy, they sell everything they have, everything they've ever placed their value in, to get the one thing that matters more than anything else.

328
00:25:59,300 --> 00:26:08,620
To take some words from Jesus, the one thing that rust won't erode, that moss can't destroy, that thieves cannot break in and steal.

329
00:26:08,620 --> 00:26:13,080
The kingdom of heaven cannot be taken from us.

330
00:26:14,200 --> 00:26:24,080
No difficult circumstance, no amount of time, no aging, no loss of income, nothing can take us.

331
00:26:24,080 --> 00:26:27,100
Nothing can take the kingdom of heaven from us.

332
00:26:27,100 --> 00:26:30,720
This is why Paul in Philippians 3, the verse that we read at worship, he says,

333
00:26:30,720 --> 00:26:37,640
It's everything else I consider rubbish, garbage, it's not worth anything because it will be taken away from me in an instant.

334
00:26:37,640 --> 00:26:44,260
All of the new gadgets and toys that we are searching after, that we are saving up for.

335
00:26:44,260 --> 00:26:51,100
I heard one pastor say, it is the yard sale of two years from now.

336
00:26:52,380 --> 00:26:59,400
Everything that you are chasing after, everything that you think will add value to your life, is going to end up in a dump somewhere.

337
00:26:59,400 --> 00:27:04,960
But the kingdom of heaven is worth the sacrifice.

338
00:27:04,960 --> 00:27:13,900
Comfort, pleasure, success, status, and significance, all of these things we chase after, all of these values we place before ourselves,

339
00:27:13,900 --> 00:27:16,520
they're all fleeting and they're outside of our control.

340
00:27:17,520 --> 00:27:26,700
No matter how self-determined, how strong-willed you think you are, no matter how good you have been at accomplishing the goals you set before yourself up until this day,

341
00:27:26,700 --> 00:27:29,240
you are not in control of everything.

342
00:27:29,240 --> 00:27:37,480
There are too many circumstances outside of your control that can break in and that can rob and that can steal and that can take from you in an instant.

343
00:27:41,400 --> 00:27:46,220
And if you don't believe me, do me a favor and read the book of Ecclesiastes this week.

344
00:27:46,220 --> 00:27:58,420
King Solomon, who has more wealth, more status, more comfort, more power, and more pleasure than all of us in the room combined times a lot.

345
00:28:00,080 --> 00:28:02,920
He writes a book at the height of his powers.

346
00:28:02,920 --> 00:28:14,240
When the nation of Israel is so powerful, so wealthy, so prosperous, when he's got all of the food, all of the women, all of the everything that he could ever want or imagine,

347
00:28:14,240 --> 00:28:17,220
he writes a book and the opening line goes something like this,

348
00:28:17,220 --> 00:28:22,980
all the works under the sun are vanity, like grabbing at the wind.

349
00:28:26,020 --> 00:28:31,220
I'll admit, it's a depressing book, if you take it at face value.

350
00:28:31,220 --> 00:28:37,220
But there's an underlying thesis behind everything that Solomon says in the book of Ecclesiastes,

351
00:28:37,220 --> 00:28:41,480
and that thesis is, there is more to life than what's under the sun.

352
00:28:41,480 --> 00:28:45,800
There is more to life than what we see on earth.

353
00:28:45,800 --> 00:28:51,580
And then a descendant of Solomon by the name of Jesus comes along, and he says,

354
00:28:51,580 --> 00:28:57,280
the kingdom of heaven is like a secure treasure worth giving up everything in pursuit of.

355
00:28:57,280 --> 00:29:00,200
See how these two stories work together?

356
00:29:00,200 --> 00:29:07,980
Jesus is saying, I know that you're pursuing comfort, pleasure, status, success, wealth,

357
00:29:08,080 --> 00:29:13,880
whatever the thing is that you thought of when I asked you to stop ignoring what I had to say,

358
00:29:13,880 --> 00:29:16,820
whatever that thing is, whatever that pursuit is,

359
00:29:16,820 --> 00:29:25,000
even if it's not necessarily sinful, there's plenty of good and meaningful and good to society things that we pursue.

360
00:29:25,000 --> 00:29:31,020
But if we pursue them as ultimate, if we're looking to find our satisfaction in them, it will fall short.

361
00:29:31,020 --> 00:29:33,180
We will be left hungry.

362
00:29:33,980 --> 00:29:35,720
We will be left thirsty.

363
00:29:35,720 --> 00:29:40,760
But there's something beyond the things of this earth.

364
00:29:40,760 --> 00:29:45,380
There's a kingdom that's worth sacrificing everything for.

365
00:29:45,380 --> 00:29:51,000
And our characters, they give up everything to receive this treasure.

366
00:29:51,000 --> 00:29:52,000
That's the last action.

367
00:29:52,000 --> 00:29:52,720
They receive it.

368
00:29:52,720 --> 00:29:58,260
But what exactly is this treasure that they have received?

369
00:29:58,260 --> 00:29:59,880
What are they giving up everything for?

370
00:29:59,880 --> 00:30:03,220
Now, hopefully we've unpacked this a little bit,

371
00:30:03,260 --> 00:30:06,360
but the question becomes, what is it that they receive?

372
00:30:06,360 --> 00:30:07,720
Easy.

373
00:30:07,720 --> 00:30:10,260
They get to go to heaven when they die.

374
00:30:10,260 --> 00:30:13,640
Come on, people.

375
00:30:13,640 --> 00:30:15,960
Seriously?

376
00:30:15,960 --> 00:30:18,620
That was a test and you all failed.

377
00:30:18,620 --> 00:30:21,400
I just preached this entire sermon.

378
00:30:21,400 --> 00:30:24,560
And then I go back to the thing that you're used to hearing.

379
00:30:24,560 --> 00:30:27,340
It's all about going to heaven when you die.

380
00:30:28,620 --> 00:30:30,420
And you guys just, you were cool.

381
00:30:30,420 --> 00:30:31,200
You were content.

382
00:30:31,200 --> 00:30:33,640
Nobody was going to stand up and yell heresy.

383
00:30:33,640 --> 00:30:40,320
Eternal life is part of the package, but it's so much more than that.

384
00:30:40,320 --> 00:30:42,140
It's freedom from our slavery to sin.

385
00:30:42,140 --> 00:30:43,600
It's abundant life now.

386
00:30:43,600 --> 00:30:48,540
It's peace that passes understanding in the worst circumstances of your life.

387
00:30:48,640 --> 00:30:53,080
It's the indwelling presence of the Holy Spirit that guides you, comforts you, prays for you,

388
00:30:53,080 --> 00:30:58,960
and transforms you into a person characterized by love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness,

389
00:30:58,960 --> 00:31:01,340
faithfulness, gentleness, and self-control.

390
00:31:01,340 --> 00:31:07,220
It's living on earth like the rule and reign of heaven is here now and is awaiting its full realization.

391
00:31:07,440 --> 00:31:09,700
And I am preaching better than you all are listening.

392
00:31:09,700 --> 00:31:16,000
I almost ran out of breath there.

393
00:31:16,000 --> 00:31:22,940
For my millennials, for my Gen Xers, the kingdom of heaven is like being red-pilled.

394
00:31:22,940 --> 00:31:31,300
It's waking up to the reality that living for any other value might be easier and more comfortable,

395
00:31:31,500 --> 00:31:33,560
but it's actually slavery and death.

396
00:31:33,560 --> 00:31:43,820
There's plenty of pursuits that are easy and comfortable and that will be valuable to our lives,

397
00:31:43,820 --> 00:31:46,560
but in the end, it's slavery and it's death.

398
00:31:46,560 --> 00:31:51,820
But the offer of the kingdom of heaven is freedom and life.

399
00:31:51,820 --> 00:31:57,420
It's being activated to live on mission with God and reconciling this broken world back to life.

400
00:31:57,420 --> 00:32:01,540
It's an invitation that is available to everyone, but will be ignored by many.

401
00:32:01,540 --> 00:32:08,220
And those who reject it will laugh at those who accept it.

402
00:32:08,220 --> 00:32:09,460
They'll think we're foolish.

403
00:32:09,460 --> 00:32:13,080
Main point.

404
00:32:13,080 --> 00:32:16,760
If you don't remember anything else, I say today, remember this.

405
00:32:16,760 --> 00:32:23,900
The kingdom of heaven is a free gift with ultimate value that requires sacrificial acceptance.

406
00:32:25,080 --> 00:32:29,200
The kingdom of heaven, it's free, it's a gift, it's offered to anyone who would take it.

407
00:32:29,200 --> 00:32:36,420
And it's more valuable than anything else you could pursue, accomplish, achieve, or receive.

408
00:32:36,420 --> 00:32:42,340
But in order to pick it up, it requires sacrificial acceptance.

409
00:32:42,340 --> 00:32:45,640
Now, some of you might protest.

410
00:32:45,640 --> 00:32:49,380
But Ryan, this sounds an awful lot like worship-based salvation.

411
00:32:50,900 --> 00:32:51,340
Right?

412
00:32:51,340 --> 00:32:52,340
Aren't we Protestant?

413
00:32:52,340 --> 00:32:55,040
Don't we believe in grace by faith alone, in Christ alone?

414
00:32:55,040 --> 00:32:58,000
What happened to this message that I've heard my whole life?

415
00:32:58,000 --> 00:33:04,180
Are you saying that if we want to be a citizen of God's kingdom, we have to work our way in or buy our way in?

416
00:33:04,180 --> 00:33:07,620
I do have a few packages to offer.

417
00:33:07,620 --> 00:33:08,200
I'm just kidding.

418
00:33:08,200 --> 00:33:11,980
I couldn't even say that one with a straight face.

419
00:33:16,220 --> 00:33:17,960
No, of course not.

420
00:33:17,960 --> 00:33:20,460
Salvation is a free gift.

421
00:33:20,460 --> 00:33:32,560
But it's a gift that when proclaimed, when Jesus proclaims the kingdom, when the apostles proclaim the kingdom, they say that if you want to be a citizen of the kingdom, you have to repent and believe.

422
00:33:34,100 --> 00:33:35,520
Repentance is turning around.

423
00:33:35,520 --> 00:33:41,260
It's setting aside what you previously held as most ultimate, as most valuable.

424
00:33:41,260 --> 00:33:46,040
And belief or faith, as the New Testament language continues, it's a new allegion.

425
00:33:46,040 --> 00:33:51,500
It's picking something else up as the ultimate value of your lives.

426
00:33:51,500 --> 00:33:58,000
Eric, do you mind coming up here?

427
00:33:58,000 --> 00:34:01,220
Sweet.

428
00:34:01,220 --> 00:34:01,780
Thank you.

429
00:34:01,780 --> 00:34:03,420
I didn't tell him this was happening.

430
00:34:03,420 --> 00:34:03,820
Huh?

431
00:34:03,820 --> 00:34:05,000
You have a sword?

432
00:34:05,000 --> 00:34:05,740
Yeah, bring the sword.

433
00:34:05,740 --> 00:34:06,220
No, I'm kidding.

434
00:34:06,220 --> 00:34:13,480
I wanted to just illustrate how I think this often works in our lives a little bit.

435
00:34:13,480 --> 00:34:16,060
And Eric is strong.

436
00:34:16,060 --> 00:34:23,160
So I figured I was going to pick your wife because she has long arms, like a wide wingspan.

437
00:34:23,160 --> 00:34:26,060
But I think I'm choosing you instead.

438
00:34:26,060 --> 00:34:26,700
It's Mother's Day.

439
00:34:26,700 --> 00:34:27,400
I didn't want to embarrass her.

440
00:34:29,560 --> 00:34:35,900
All right, let's, I'm going to take a guess at what I think Eric might find as ultimate value in his life.

441
00:34:37,300 --> 00:34:49,680
